Good, that's exactly the trap the finite-layer solution avoids — and
it's worth being precise about why. The differential form dI/dz = -I +
B is a local relation; integrating it naively (or worse, differencing
it) runs into exactly the instability you hit, because it doesn't know
it's supposed to be integrated analytically over a homogeneous
layer. The finite-layer solution you're describing is the standard
closed-form integral of that same equation assuming constant (or
linear-in-τ) Planck function within the layer:

  I_out = I_in * t_k + B_k * (1 - t_k)          where t_k = exp(-tau_k / mu)

This is unconditionally stable regardless of lapse-rate sign — it
doesn't care whether you're in the troposphere or stratosphere,
because it's not stepping a derivative, it's applying the exact layer
solution directly. This is what I'll use for composition.

One structural thing worth flagging before the code: tau has to be
per-channel, not a single scalar per layer, since absorption differs
channel-to-channel even within one band. So the network's output isn't
"one number per layer" — it's a vector of optical depths across all
channels in the band, per layer. Composition then loops over layers,
and at each layer does an elementwise (per-channel) blend.

