Straight answer: as written, no — it likely won't fix the
layer-by-layer problem, for exactly the reason you just
named. Training against TOA radiance only gives you one scalar
constraint per channel per profile. There are many different
τ_k(k=1...N) layer sequences that compose to the same correct TOA
radiance — the composition is many-to-one. Nothing in the loss I wrote
distinguishes among them. So tauNet is free to converge to a solution
that nails TOA, while still being "almost but not completely correct"
layer-by-layer — which is precisely the SARTA failure mode you're
trying to escape, just now wearing a neural network instead of a
regression. I gave you a differentiable composition, but I didn't give
you anything that breaks the non-uniqueness, and that's the actual
hard part of this problem. Worth saying plainly rather than letting
you find that out after a training run.

Testing it as-is is still worth doing — it'll tell you whether the
composition mechanics even work (finite, stable, no NaNs, trains
without diverging), which is a necessary first step regardless. But go
in expecting the TOA fit to look great and the per-layer values to
still be suspect, and treat that as confirming the diagnosis, not as a
failure of the code.

To actually break the degeneracy, you need a second source of
constraint. Given what you have access to, two realistic options:

1. Regularize toward SARTA's own current per-layer optical depths as a
prior, rather than letting tauNet roam freely. SARTA's layer values
are already close — your whole premise is that SARTA is a good
approximation with only small internal layer errors. So add a penalty
term:

  loss = MSE(R_TOA_predicted, R_TOA_true) + lambda * mean((tau_k_predicted - tau_k_SARTA).^2)

This tells the network: "match KCARTA at TOA, but don't wander from
SARTA's layer values unless the TOA mismatch actually requires it."
That directly targets your stated goal — find where SARTA's internal
layers are wrong — instead of letting the optimizer find an arbitrary
alternate solution that also happens to hit TOA.

2. If you can generate them, intermediate-level radiances are the real
fix, not a workaround. If KCARTA (or even a modified SARTA run) can
give you "radiance as if the atmosphere above level k were the only
thing present, with level k as an opaque surface" for a handful of
levels (say every 10th layer) — that directly supervises the
composition at multiple points along the path, not just at the very
top. Even 5-10 extra intermediate constraints per profile would
collapse a huge amount of the degeneracy. This is more RT-code work
than option 1, but it's the more rigorous fix.

