As written, it assumes fixed nLayers — the for k = 1:nLayers loop
bound and layerTempAll (nLayers x batch) both require every profile in
a batch to have the same layer count. It won't handle your actual
variable-N profiles directly.

Worth noting though: this is actually a case where you don't need
sigma-grid interpolation at all, and arguably shouldn't use it. The
whole reason you moved to local per-layer predictors was to stop
smoothing away exactly the kind of local structure global EOF
compression discards — re-interpolating onto a fixed grid here would
partially undo that. Since each layer's optical depth only depends on
that layer's own local predictors (not on where it sits relative to a
fixed reference grid), there's no structural reason the network needs
a common grid at all.

Fix: pad to a common nLayersMax within each batch, and mask the
padding layers to be physically transparent (τ=0) rather than
interpolating anything. A τ=0 layer passes radiance through completely
unchanged (t_k = exp(0) = 1, so I = I*1 + B*0 = I), which is exactly
correct — it's equivalent to "no atmosphere here," which is physically
true for a profile whose real levels stop above nLayersMax. Native
pressures/temperatures are used unmodified for every real layer.

    nLayersMax = 100;   % pad up to this; real N varies 75-100 per your terrain discussion
    
    % per-profile: layerFeaturesAll{k} and layerTempAll(k,:) for k > actualN(profile)
    % get set to values that force tau=0 -- simplest is a dedicated mask, applied
    % AFTER tauNet rather than relying on the input features alone to force tau=0:
    
    function tau = forwardLayerODMasked(params, layerFeatures, validMask)
        % layerFeatures: (11 x batch); validMask: (1 x batch), 1=real layer, 0=padding
        raw = fullyconnect(relu(fullyconnect(layerFeatures, params.tau_fc1_W, params.tau_fc1_b)), ...
                            params.tau_fc2_W, params.tau_fc2_b);
        raw = fullyconnect(raw, params.tau_fc3_W, params.tau_fc3_b);
        tau = stableSoftplus(raw);
        tau = tau .* validMask;   % force tau=0 for padded/nonexistent layers -- physically "no atmosphere"
    end

Inside composeRTUpward, pass validMask(k,:) through at each layer's
tauNet call, built once per profile as validMask(k,i) = (k <=
actualN(i)). Padded layers then contribute nothing to the composed
radiance regardless of what garbage sits in their input features
(you'd just fill those with zeros or the last valid layer's values —
doesn't matter, since they're multiplied out).

This sidesteps interpolation loss entirely for the
layer-composition/effective-OD work, while your existing sigma-grid
pipeline stays exactly as-is for the global EOF radiance network. Want
me to fold this masking into the full script?

